/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Binary Tree Right Side View
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Binary Tree, BFS, Queue
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given the root of a binary tree, imagine standing on the
right side of the tree.

Return the values of the nodes that are visible from the
right side, ordered from top to bottom.

Only one node can be visible from each level.

Example:

    root = [1,2,3,null,5,null,4]

            1
           / \
          2   3
           \   \
            5   4

From the right side, we can see:

    1
    3
    4

Output:

    [1,3,4]

Approach:
Use Breadth First Search (BFS) with a Queue.

Since the right side view contains at most one node from
each level, we process the tree level by level.

For every level:
1. Store the number of nodes in that level.
2. Process all nodes of the current level.
3. The last node processed at that level is the rightmost
   node.
4. Add its value to the answer.

Because we push children in this order:

    left child
    right child

the rightmost node will be the last node removed from the
queue for that level.

------------------------------------------------------------
Example:
------------------------------------------------------------

    Tree:

            1
           / \
          2   3
           \   \
            5   4

Initially:

    Queue = [1]

Level 1:

    Process 1

    Last node of level = 1

    Answer:
        [1]

    Queue = [2,3]

Level 2:

    Process 2
    Process 3

    Last node of level = 3

    Answer:
        [1,3]

    Queue = [5,4]

Level 3:

    Process 5
    Process 4

    Last node of level = 4

    Answer:
        [1,3,4]

------------------------------------------------------------
Why does the last node represent the right side view?
------------------------------------------------------------

At every level, BFS processes nodes from left to right.

Therefore, the last node processed at that level is the
rightmost node.

The rightmost node is the one visible when looking at the
tree from the right side.

------------------------------------------------------------
Alternative BFS Approach:
------------------------------------------------------------

We could also push the right child before the left child:

    right
    left

Then the first node of every level would be the visible node.

However, your approach is simpler because it keeps the
standard left-to-right BFS order and selects the last node.

------------------------------------------------------------
Example with Missing Right Children:
------------------------------------------------------------

    root = [1,2,3,4,null,null,5]

            1
           / \
          2   3
         /     \
        4       5

Right side view:

    [1,3,5]

Even though node 2 is present, node 3 is farther to the
right at the same level, so node 3 is visible.

------------------------------------------------------------
Algorithm:
------------------------------------------------------------

1. If root is nullptr, return an empty result.
2. Push root into a queue.
3. While the queue is not empty:
      - Store the current level size.
      - Process exactly that many nodes.
      - If the current node is the last node of the level,
        add its value to the result.
      - Push its left and right children.
4. Return the result.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
Every node is inserted into and removed from the queue exactly
once.

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The queue can contain O(n) nodes in the worst case.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:

    vector<int> rightSideView(TreeNode* root) {

        vector<int> ans;

        // Empty tree
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return ans;
        }

        queue<TreeNode*> q;

        q.push(root);

        while (!q.empty()) {

            // Number of nodes in the current level
            int levelSize = q.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {

                TreeNode* node = q.front();
                q.pop();

                // Last node of the current level
                // is visible from the right side.
                if (i == levelSize - 1) {
                    ans.push_back(node->val);
                }

                // Add left child
                if (node->left != nullptr) {
                    q.push(node->left);
                }

                // Add right child
                if (node->right != nullptr) {
                    q.push(node->right);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
